course: dictionary
lesson: 2207
title: Counterweight: the heavy balancer
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A counterweight is a heavy weight that balances another weight. Read the clues around the word. «A crane uses pulleys, a long arm and a heavy counterweight that balances the load like a seesaw.» A seesaw balances when two people sit at each end, so a counterweight must be the weight on the other side that stops the load pulling the crane over.
explain: Now test your meaning. Swap «balances» for «a heavy weight that stops the load tipping» and read the sentence again. Does it still make sense? It does, so you are on the right track.
explain: The word also works in a figure-of-speech way. «Cranes and lifts use a heavy counterweight like a seesaw partner.» If one idea balances another idea, we can call it a counterweight too. Look for clues such as «to» or «against» that show what is being balanced.
fact: A counterweight is a heavy weight used to balance another weight.
fact: «A counterweight» can help a heavy thing, such as a load, become easier to lift.
fact: «A forklift truck» has a heavy counterweight at the back to stop it tipping forward.
fact: «Tower Bridge in London» has opened for ships since 1894, and lifting bridges use counterweights to lift or turn.
fact: Figuratively, a counterweight can be an idea or thing that balances something else, as in «a counterweight to».
exercise: «A crane has a big concrete counterweight at the back so it does not fall over.» What is the counterweight for? | To play music | To make the crane fall over on purpose | To hold the people who work there | *To balance the load so the crane does not tip
exercise: «One idea can act as a counterweight to another idea.» What does counterweight mean in this sentence? | A type of bridge over a river | An argument that ends the talk | Something very light that floats | *Something that balances another
exercise: «Lifting and swing bridges use motors and counterweights so tall ships can pass.» Find and copy the word that names what needs to pass the bridge. | motors | London | counterweights | *ships
test: «Lifts have a counterweight going down as the lift goes up.» What happens to the counterweight as the lift goes up? | It goes up too | *It goes down | It turns into a cable | It stops working
test: «A heavy counterweight at the back stops a forklift tipping forward.» Why is the counterweight at the back? | To make the forklift faster | To keep people away from the walkway | *To stop it tipping forward | To hold the forks
exam: «The kind teacher was a counterweight to the strict rules.» What does counterweight mean here? | The end of all the rules | A heavy weight that lifts the teacher | *Something that balances the strict rules | A rule for ships
